public class ConstructorDemo {
    private final String name;
    private final int age;

    ConstructorDemo() {
        this("Unknown");
        System.out.println("No-argument constructor");
    }

    ConstructorDemo(String name) {
        this(name, 0);
        System.out.println("One-argument constructor");
    }

    ConstructorDemo(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
        System.out.println("Two-argument constructor");
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Name: " + name + ", age: " + age);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ConstructorDemo first = new ConstructorDemo();
        first.display();

        System.out.println();

        ConstructorDemo second = new ConstructorDemo("Asha", 20);
        second.display();
    }
}
# OOP in Java - Lecture 2: Constructors

The video is titled **"Constructors in Java | Constructor Chaining, Overloading & `this`"**. These notes summarize those topics; they are not a verbatim transcript.

## Constructors

- A constructor initializes a newly created object.
- Its name must exactly match the class name, and it has no return type (not even `void`).
- It runs when an object is created with `new`.
- If a class declares no constructors, Java supplies a no-argument default constructor. Once any constructor is declared, Java does not supply that one automatically.

## Constructor overloading

A class can declare multiple constructors as long as their parameter lists differ in number, types, or order. This lets callers initialize objects in different ways. A different return type alone does not make a constructor overload; constructors have no return types.

## `this`

- `this` refers to the current object. It is useful when a parameter has the same name as an instance field, for example `this.name = name`.
- `this(...)` calls another constructor in the same class, allowing initialization logic to be reused.
- A `this(...)` constructor call must be the first statement in its constructor. A constructor cannot directly or indirectly chain back to itself.

## Constructor chaining

In the example, the no-argument constructor delegates to the one-argument constructor, which delegates to the two-argument constructor. The two-argument constructor performs the actual field initialization. This keeps the initialization logic in one place.

Run `ConstructorDemo.java` to see the chained and overloaded constructors in action.
